WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:08.221
Back in the warehouse, the last video's crew, G1, had one habit nobody could miss.

2
00:00:08.221 --> 00:00:15.717
Every time it moved boxes, it called pens down, and every worker waited until the last box sat on its new shelf.

3
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:18.809
On a small floor, that takes a few milliseconds.

4
00:00:18.809 --> 00:00:21.690
But the wait grows with the boxes still in use.

5
00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:27.023
Give the thumbnail service a cache of many gigabytes, and every pause has more to copy.

6
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:30.801
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:30.801 --> 00:00:34.138
Could the crew move boxes while the workers keep working?

8
00:00:34.138 --> 00:00:40.049
And if it moves my object while my thread is using it, why doesn't my thread walk to the wrong shelf?

9
00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:44.467
Moving furniture while people are still sitting on it sounds like a prank.

10
00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:46.851
Let's see how a crew could do it politely.

11
00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:52.547
Picture a crew that leaves a forwarding note each time it moves a box.

12
00:00:52.547 --> 00:00:54.542
This box is now on shelf 812.

13
00:00:54.542 --> 00:01:00.937
A worker who follows an old shelf number reads the note once, and corrects their own notepad.

14
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:05.137
But how does a worker know a shelf number might be old?

15
00:01:05.137 --> 00:01:08.586
Every number on the notepad wears a small colored sticker.

16
00:01:08.586 --> 00:01:10.846
The crew announces today's good color.

17
00:01:10.846 --> 00:01:13.819
A number with any other color needs a second look.

18
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:14.851
Let's map it.

19
00:01:14.851 --> 00:01:16.095
A box is an object.

20
00:01:16.095 --> 00:01:20.481
A shelf number on the notepad is a reference, the object's address.

21
00:01:20.481 --> 00:01:24.802
The sticker is a few extra bits inside every reference, its color.

22
00:01:24.802 --> 00:01:31.676
And the second look is a few instructions the JIT adds after every load of a reference: the load barrier.

23
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.668
Here's the barrier, in spirit.

24
00:01:34.668 --> 00:01:36.668
Your line loads cache.latest.

25
00:01:36.668 --> 00:01:39.770
The barrier adds one test: is its color good?

26
00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:43.976
Nearly always it is, and the cost is one test and one branch.

27
00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:47.480
Now the crew changes the good color, and starts moving.

28
00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.488
Your thread loads the same field.

29
00:01:49.488 --> 00:01:52.713
Its color is old, so the barrier takes the slow path.

30
00:01:52.713 --> 00:02:00.928
It finds the new address, moves the object itself if the crew hasn't got to it yet, and writes the fixed reference back into the field.

31
00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:03.790
So, the next load from that same field.

32
00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.047
What does it cost?

33
00:02:05.047 --> 00:02:06.164
Pause and guess.

34
00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:12.821
One test and one branch again, because the color is good now.

35
00:02:12.821 --> 00:02:17.365
The worker read the forwarding note once, and fixed the shelf number for good.

36
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:20.449
The picture bends in two places.

37
00:02:20.449 --> 00:02:23.654
ZGC doesn't leave its notes on the old shelves.

38
00:02:23.654 --> 00:02:31.087
It keeps them in a small table beside each area it empties, so the old shelves can take new boxes right away.

39
00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:35.832
And a real worker reads a note only after walking to an old shelf.

40
00:02:35.832 --> 00:02:41.712
ZGC's check runs on every reference load, all day, whether a move is going on or not.

41
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.374
The colors need room.

42
00:02:44.374 --> 00:02:49.357
So ZGC doesn't use lesson 1's compressed 4-byte references.

43
00:02:49.357 --> 00:02:51.300
Each one takes 8 bytes.

44
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:56.790
The lesson's Thumbnail takes 40 bytes under G1, and 48 under ZGC.

45
00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:02.196
The allocation counter agrees: 10,000 thumbnails, 480,000 bytes.

46
00:03:03.067 --> 00:03:12.724
Now let's read one collection from the log: a JDK 25 run of the demo's load, on a busy 4-core machine.

47
00:03:12.724 --> 00:03:17.647
A minor collection cleans only the young generation.

48
00:03:17.647 --> 00:03:22.287
The last line says 852 MB down to 176, in 0.071s.

49
00:03:22.467 --> 00:03:24.919
So how long were your threads stopped?

50
00:03:24.919 --> 00:03:26.532
Seventy-one milliseconds?

51
00:03:26.532 --> 00:03:27.564
Pause and guess.

52
00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:31.059
No.

53
00:03:31.059 --> 00:03:39.820
The 0.071s is the whole collection, and it ran beside your program.

54
00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:48.057
The stops are the three Pause lines: 0.011, 0.015 and 0.020 ms.

55
00:03:48.057 --> 00:03:50.149
Together, 0.046.

56
00:03:50.333 --> 00:03:55.955
Seventy-one milliseconds of cleaning, and the workers lost less than a twentieth of one.

57
00:03:55.955 --> 00:03:58.319
That's quicker than saying pens down.

58
00:03:58.319 --> 00:04:02.727
So the point is: in a ZGC log, the collection time is not your pause.

59
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:04.504
Now the names.

60
00:04:04.504 --> 00:04:11.732
ZGC, the Z Garbage Collector, does concurrent relocation: it moves live objects while your threads keep running.

61
00:04:11.732 --> 00:04:14.314
The sticker is called a colored pointer.

62
00:04:14.314 --> 00:04:21.995
The pauses only switch the crew from one step to the next, and the java manual says they don't depend on the heap size.

63
00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:24.674
Watch your flags, though.

64
00:04:24.674 --> 00:04:29.044
JDK 21 added generational ZGC as an option.

65
00:04:29.044 --> 00:04:35.143
JDK 23 made it the default, and JDK 24 removed the old mode.

66
00:04:35.143 --> 00:04:40.123
So on JDK 25, -XX:+UseZGC means generational ZGC.

67
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.013
So the pauses are nearly gone.

68
00:04:43.013 --> 00:04:44.020
The work isn't.

69
00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:49.255
Somebody still marks and moves every live box, and somebody runs that barrier.

70
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.208
The bill has three parts.

71
00:04:51.208 --> 00:04:54.174
CPU: the crew's threads run beside yours.

72
00:04:54.174 --> 00:05:00.684
Headroom: if the floor fills before the crew finishes, threads that want memory must wait.

73
00:05:00.684 --> 00:05:02.637
That's an allocation stall.

74
00:05:02.637 --> 00:05:08.063
In a 96 MB heap, the demo logged 22 of them in one run, and 30 in the next.

75
00:05:08.063 --> 00:05:10.667
And memory: those 8-byte references.

76
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.080
So the bill has a forwarding note too.

77
00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.842
Short pauses move the work.

78
00:05:15.842 --> 00:05:17.212
They don't remove it.

79
00:05:17.212 --> 00:05:18.517
Check where it went.

80
00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:22.426
So why doesn't your thread walk to the wrong shelf?

81
00:05:22.426 --> 00:05:26.757
Every reference wears a color, and a barrier glances at it on every load.

82
00:05:26.757 --> 00:05:30.139
A stale one gets fixed once, by the thread that found it.

83
00:05:30.139 --> 00:05:33.165
The crew moves boxes, and the workers keep working.

84
00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:34.967
Quick check.

85
00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:40.669
Your service runs in a container with 2 CPUs, and it's busiest at lunchtime.

86
00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:42.769
Are ZGC's short pauses free?

87
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:46.231
No.

88
00:05:46.231 --> 00:05:52.698
ZGC's threads compete with your request threads exactly when the service is busiest.

89
00:05:52.698 --> 00:05:55.855
Watch the CPU, and the allocation stalls.
